From: Mirdispo contributors
Subject: Raise the x264 pipeline latency to 120 ms so frames leave evenly

Patch 0021 lowered the x264 pipeline latency to 80 ms after measuring when
the first packet of each frame left. The last packets do not leave with it:
with alignment 7 the muxer keeps the tail of each frame until its next input
arrives, a burst of sound every 40 ms or the next frame. At 80 ms those tails
miss their time at the sink, so they go out whenever the muxer releases them
and frames arrive unevenly, which a receiver shows as stutter. Measured from
the last packet of each frame with real sound capture, the time between
frames ranged from 7 to 94 ms at 80 ms, with the same spread on the engine
before 0021, and was 33.3 ms with under a millisecond of spread at 100, 120
and 150 ms.

The latency is now 120 ms, which keeps frames even with room to spare and
still sends them sooner than the 150 ms of patch 0017.

diff -ruN a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
--- a/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
+++ b/src/wfd/wfd-media-factory.c
@@ -584,11 +584,14 @@
    * Every packet is sent that much after it was captured, so the latency is
    * felt as the receiver lagging behind the desktop. x264 is configured for
    * zero latency with no lookahead and no B-frames, and does not have those
-   * spikes, so it gets a much shorter one: measured with sound included,
-   * 80 ms sends a frame about 35 ms after capture, where 150 ms took over
-   * 100 ms, and the frames leave no less evenly. */
+   * spikes, so it gets a shorter one. Not too short: the muxer holds the
+   * last packets of each frame until its next input arrives, and below about
+   * 100 ms those tails miss their time at the sink and leave whenever the
+   * muxer lets them go. Measured from the last packet of each frame, the
+   * time between frames was 33.3 ms with a spread of under a millisecond at
+   * 100 to 150 ms, and ranged from 7 to 94 ms at 80 ms. */
   if (encoder == ELEMENT_X264)
-    gst_pipeline_set_latency (GST_PIPELINE (pipeline), 80 * GST_MSECOND);
+    gst_pipeline_set_latency (GST_PIPELINE (pipeline), 120 * GST_MSECOND);
   else
     gst_pipeline_set_latency (GST_PIPELINE (pipeline), 500 * GST_MSECOND);
 
